\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ threshold as a function of the assumed transmitter linewidth $\Delta \nu '$, for three representative windows (coloured curves) and the envelope of all 225 window limits of this survey (grey band): the UV\nobreakspace  {}Ceti Band\nobreakspace  {}3 window has 15.625-MHz channels (the worked example above), the $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris Band\nobreakspace  {}6 fine window has 15.3-kHz channels, and the TRAPPIST-1 Band\nobreakspace  {}6 window has 31.25-MHz channels. Each curve is flat at the quoted limit for any carrier narrower than the window's native channel width $\Delta \nu _{\rm  ch}$ (dot at each knee); above $\Delta \nu _{\rm  ch}$ the required power rises linearly with linewidth. The grey dotted curve shows the deepest window under an idealised re-channelisation, for which the threshold would fall as $\sqrt  {\Delta \nu '}$. The shaded vertical band marks the Hz-scale channel widths of L-band technosignature backends, within which every curve of this survey is in its flat regime. The green dashed line is an Arecibo-like planetary radar ($2\times 10^{13}$\,W).}}{23}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:linewidth}{{7}{23}{EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ threshold as a function of the assumed transmitter linewidth $\Delta \nu '$, for three representative windows (coloured curves) and the envelope of all 225 window limits of this survey (grey band): the UV~Ceti Band~3 window has 15.625-MHz channels (the worked example above), the $\beta $~Pictoris Band~6 fine window has 15.3-kHz channels, and the TRAPPIST-1 Band~6 window has 31.25-MHz channels. Each curve is flat at the quoted limit for any carrier narrower than the window's native channel width $\Delta \nu _{\rm ch}$ (dot at each knee); above $\Delta \nu _{\rm ch}$ the required power rises linearly with linewidth. The grey dotted curve shows the deepest window under an idealised re-channelisation, for which the threshold would fall as $\sqrt {\Delta \nu '}$. The shaded vertical band marks the Hz-scale channel widths of L-band technosignature backends, within which every curve of this survey is in its flat regime. The green dashed line is an Arecibo-like planetary radar ($2\times 10^{13}$\,W)}{figure.7}{}}
